/* Evaluation and Testing — 31 questions */
window.QA_SECTION = {
  id: '09',
  slug: 'evaluation',
  title: 'Evaluation & Testing',
  blurb: 'Eval-driven development, LLM judges, red teaming, regression suites, and statistical rigor.',
  questions: [
{ q: 'What is LLM evaluation, and what makes it different?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `LLM evaluation is measuring whether a system built on a language model does what you need — and it breaks most assumptions carried over from classical ML evaluation.<br><br><strong>What changes:</strong> there is usually <em>no single correct output</em>, so accuracy against a labeled test set does not apply to open-ended generation. Outputs are <em>non-deterministic</em>, so a single run is a sample, not a measurement. The thing under test is a <em>system</em> — prompt, retrieval, tools, guardrails — not a model you trained, and the model underneath can change without a deploy on your side. And evaluation <em>costs money</em> per example, which constrains how often you can run it.<br><br><strong>The layered approach that works:</strong> deterministic checks wherever the task admits them (exact match, schema validity, code execution) because they are free and unambiguous; reference-based metrics as weak directional signals; LLM-as-judge for open-ended output, <em>validated against human labels</em>; and human review as the ground truth calibrating everything above it.<br><br><strong>The structural requirements:</strong> a golden set built from real traffic that oversamples failures, a held-out slice you never iterate against, per-category reporting (aggregates hide one category collapsing), and multiple runs with variance reported rather than a single number.<br><br><strong>The point that matters most:</strong> public benchmark performance correlates weakly with performance on your task, and contamination is widespread. Evaluate on your own data or you are measuring someone else's problem.` },

{ q: 'How do you evaluate AI agents?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Evaluate at three levels, because a single end-to-end score is not actionable.</strong><br><br><strong>1. Outcome</strong> — did the task succeed? Use a programmatic check where possible (tests pass, correct record created, file matches expected) rather than an LLM judge. Report success rate <em>alongside</em> cost per task, steps per task, and latency: an agent succeeding 90% of the time in 40 steps is not better than one succeeding 85% in 6.<br><br><strong>2. Trajectory</strong> — was the path sensible? Tool-selection accuracy, parameter correctness, redundant or repeated calls, and recovery rate after errors. This is where you find <em>why</em> outcomes fail. An agent can reach the right answer through a wasteful or dangerous path, and outcome-only evaluation hides that entirely.<br><br><strong>3. Component</strong> — unit-test the deterministic pieces: does the router classify correctly, does the tool schema validate, does retrieval recall the right documents. Mock the tools so these run fast in CI.<br><br><strong>What makes agent evaluation hard:</strong> non-determinism means you need multiple runs per case and should report pass rate with variance, not a single number. Long trajectories compound errors, so per-step accuracy matters. And there are usually many valid paths, so never assert an exact trajectory — assert <em>invariants</em> instead ("never called delete", "cited a real source", "stayed under budget").<br><br><strong>Build the eval set from real failures</strong> mined from production traces, oversampling hard cases, and run it as a regression suite on every prompt, tool, or model change.` },

{ q: 'How do you evaluate LLM outputs generally?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Match the method to the task, cheapest first.</strong><br><br><strong>Deterministic evaluation</strong> where a correct answer exists: exact match, F1, schema validity, code execution, numeric comparison. Fast, free, reliable, and reproducible. Use it wherever the task admits it — teams reach for LLM judges far too early.<br><br><strong>Reference-based metrics</strong> (BLEU, ROUGE, BERTScore) when you have gold outputs — but understand they measure surface overlap, not correctness. A fluent fabrication can score well on ROUGE, which is why they are unsuitable for faithfulness.<br><br><strong>LLM-as-judge</strong> for open-ended generation, with a concrete rubric and few-shot calibration examples. <em>Validate the judge against human labels before trusting it</em> — an uncalibrated judge is a random number generator with good prose. Prefer pairwise comparison ("which is better") over absolute 1–10 scoring; judges are far more reliable at comparison, and you must randomize position to control for position bias.<br><br><strong>Human evaluation</strong> as the ground truth that validates everything else, on a sampled basis.<br><br><strong>The structural advice that matters more than metric choice:</strong> build the eval set <em>before</em> writing prompts, source it from real traffic oversampling failures, split dev and held-out so you do not overfit, and report variance across multiple runs since output is non-deterministic.<br><br><strong>And always measure cost and latency alongside quality</strong> — a 2% quality gain at 3x cost is usually a bad trade, and quality-only reporting hides it.` },

{ q: 'What is AI agent observability?', tags: [], a: `Agent observability is the ability to reconstruct <em>why</em> an agent did what it did — not just that a task failed. It is harder than conventional observability because the control flow is model-decided and non-deterministic, so there is no stack trace to read.<br><br><strong>The unit is the trajectory trace:</strong> a tree of spans covering every step — the model's reasoning output, the tool call with arguments, the observation returned, and the resulting state change. Each span carries latency, tokens, and cost.<br><br><strong>What must be captured, and what teams miss:</strong><br>• The <strong>fully rendered prompt</strong> at each step, not the template. Context assembly bugs — a truncated observation, an empty variable, a compaction that dropped the task definition — are the most common cause of inexplicable agent behavior, and they are invisible without the literal string.<br>• Tool arguments <em>and</em> results, including errors verbatim. An unactionable error message is a frequent root cause of loops.<br>• Cumulative budget state (steps, tokens, cost) so you can see where a run went off the rails.<br>• Guardrail triggers and their inputs.<br><br><strong>What this enables:</strong> replaying a failed trajectory against a modified prompt or tool definition; converting any production failure into an eval case; and diagnosing whether a failure was reasoning, tool design, or context assembly — three very different fixes.<br><br><strong>Aggregate views matter too:</strong> tool-selection distribution, per-tool error rates, step-count distribution, and cost tails. A single tool with a high validation-failure rate is a definition problem you can fix in minutes — but only if you can see it.` },

{ q: 'What is evaluation-driven development for AI applications?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Evaluation-driven development means building the eval set <em>before</em> the implementation, and treating it as the definition of done — the AI analogue of test-driven development.<br><br><strong>The workflow:</strong> define what good looks like with concrete examples → build a labeled eval set → establish a baseline → iterate on prompts, retrieval, and models against that set → ship when the metric clears the bar → keep the set as a regression suite.<br><br><strong>Why it matters more here than in conventional software:</strong> LLM behavior is not inspectable by reading the code. You cannot reason your way to whether a prompt change helped — the only way to know is to measure. Teams without evals iterate on vibes, and the characteristic failure is a sequence of changes that each felt like improvements and collectively made the system worse, with no way to detect it.<br><br><strong>The second reason:</strong> your dependencies change underneath you. A provider model update alters behavior with no commit on your side, so continuous evaluation is the only mechanism that catches it.<br><br><strong>What a good eval set looks like:</strong> sourced from real traffic, deliberately oversampling failures and edge cases rather than the easy average, 50–200 examples to start, split into dev and held-out so you do not overfit to the examples you keep reading.<br><br><strong>The organizational point:</strong> the hardest part is not tooling, it is getting agreement on what "good" means precisely enough to measure. That forced specificity is itself most of the value — much of what looks like a modeling problem turns out to be an undefined-requirements problem.` },

{ q: 'Explain BLEU, ROUGE, and BERTScore. When would you use each?', tags: [], a: `<strong>BLEU</strong> — precision-oriented n-gram overlap with the reference, plus a brevity penalty. Built for machine translation, where the output space is constrained and there is a fairly canonical correct answer.<br><br><strong>ROUGE</strong> — recall-oriented n-gram overlap. ROUGE-N for n-grams, ROUGE-L for longest common subsequence. Built for summarization, asking how much of the reference content the output covers.<br><br><strong>BERTScore</strong> — token-level similarity using contextual embeddings rather than exact string matching, so paraphrases score well where BLEU and ROUGE penalize them. Correlates better with human judgment than either.<br><br><strong>The limitation that governs all three, and the point to lead with:</strong> they measure similarity to a reference, not correctness. A fluent fabrication that reuses the source's vocabulary can score high on ROUGE. A correct answer phrased differently from the reference scores low. <strong>None of them detect hallucination</strong> — teams reporting ROUGE while shipping a hallucinating summarizer are measuring the wrong thing entirely, and that is a common real failure.<br><br><strong>When to use them:</strong> BLEU for translation with good references. ROUGE as a coarse coverage signal for summarization, never as a quality metric alone. BERTScore where you want reference-based scoring tolerant of paraphrase.<br><br><strong>What to use instead for modern LLM work:</strong> task-specific deterministic checks where possible, faithfulness metrics (NLI-based entailment, AlignScore, SummaC) for groundedness, and calibrated LLM judges for open-ended quality. Reference-based n-gram metrics are largely legacy for generative evaluation.` },

{ q: 'What is G-Eval, and how does it use LLMs for evaluation?', tags: [], a: `G-Eval is a framework for LLM-as-judge evaluation with two distinguishing ideas.<br><br><strong>1. Chain-of-thought evaluation steps.</strong> Rather than asking the judge to score directly, G-Eval first has the model generate the evaluation <em>steps</em> from the criteria definition (auto-CoT), then applies those steps to produce a score. Making the evaluation procedure explicit improves consistency and correlation with human judgment.<br><br><strong>2. Probability-weighted scoring.</strong> Instead of taking the emitted score token, it reads the model's probability distribution over the score tokens (1–5) and computes the expectation. This solves a real problem: raw LLM scores are coarse and heavily clustered — judges emit "4" constantly — so tiny quality differences are invisible. Weighting by probability gives a continuous score that discriminates between outputs a discrete rating cannot separate.<br><br><strong>Reported results</strong> show notably higher correlation with human judgment on summarization tasks than prior reference-based and judge approaches.<br><br><strong>The practical constraint:</strong> probability-weighted scoring requires logprob access, which not every provider exposes — so many implementations fall back to sampling multiple scores and averaging, which approximates the same effect at higher cost.<br><br><strong>It inherits every LLM-judge limitation:</strong> position bias, verbosity bias, self-preference for outputs from the same model family, and sensitivity to rubric phrasing. G-Eval improves the scoring mechanism; it does not make the judge objective. Validate against human labels before trusting it, and prefer pairwise comparison where the task allows.` },

{ q: 'What is LLM-as-a-judge, and what are its limitations?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `LLM-as-a-judge uses a model to score or compare outputs against a rubric, providing scalable evaluation for open-ended generation where no reference answer exists and human review does not scale.<br><br><strong>The known biases, which are the substance of this question:</strong><br>• <em>Position bias</em> — in pairwise comparison, judges systematically favor one position. Mitigate by evaluating both orderings and averaging.<br>• <em>Verbosity bias</em> — longer answers score higher regardless of quality. Control by normalizing for length or explicitly instructing against it.<br>• <em>Self-preference</em> — models rate their own outputs (and their family's) higher. Use a different model family as judge than the one being evaluated.<br>• <em>Poor calibration on absolute scales</em> — 1–10 scores cluster tightly and mean little across contexts. Pairwise comparison is substantially more reliable.<br>• <em>Sycophancy</em> — judges agree with confident-sounding assertions.<br>• <em>Weak on correctness</em> — judges are much better at style, coherence, and instruction-following than at detecting subtle factual errors, especially in specialized domains.<br><br><strong>The essential practice:</strong> validate the judge against human labels before trusting it. Measure agreement on a few hundred human-scored examples. An unvalidated judge produces confident numbers that may correlate with nothing — and teams optimize against those numbers for months.<br><br><strong>Use it correctly:</strong> concrete rubrics with worked examples, pairwise over absolute, randomized position, a strong model as judge, and periodic human spot-checks to detect drift. It is a scalable proxy for human judgment, not a replacement for it.` },

{ q: 'How do you conduct human evaluation for AI systems?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Define the criteria precisely first.</strong> "Is this good?" produces noise. Break quality into specific dimensions — factual accuracy, completeness, instruction adherence, tone, safety — and rate each separately. Vague criteria are the largest single source of low inter-annotator agreement.<br><br><strong>Prefer comparison over rating.</strong> Humans are far more consistent at "which of these two is better" than at "rate this 1–10," and pairwise results can be aggregated into rankings (Elo, Bradley-Terry). Absolute scales drift between annotators and over time.<br><br><strong>Control for bias:</strong> blind annotators to which system produced which output, randomize presentation order, and randomize example order to counter fatigue effects.<br><br><strong>Measure agreement, not just scores.</strong> Report Krippendorff's alpha or Cohen's kappa — raw agreement is inflated by chance. Low agreement means either your criteria are ambiguous or the examples are genuinely ambiguous, and those need different fixes. Use multiple annotators per example on a subset to quantify this.<br><br><strong>Run calibration sessions:</strong> have annotators label the same set, discuss divergences, and refine the guidelines. This is the highest-leverage investment in annotation quality and is routinely skipped.<br><br><strong>Include gold-standard items</strong> embedded in the stream to monitor annotator quality continuously — quality drifts over time.<br><br><strong>Use human evaluation strategically:</strong> it is expensive and slow, so use it to <em>validate</em> automated metrics and judges, then rely on those for iteration, returning to humans periodically to confirm the proxy still tracks.<br><br><strong>Domain expertise matters:</strong> in medical, legal, or financial applications, non-expert annotation is close to worthless.` },

{ q: 'What is red teaming, and how do you red team an LLM application?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `Red teaming is adversarial testing — deliberately attempting to make the system produce harmful, incorrect, or policy-violating output, to find failure modes before users or attackers do.<br><br><strong>Attack categories to cover systematically:</strong><br>• <em>Jailbreaks</em> — role-play personas, hypothetical framing, gradual escalation across turns, encoding/obfuscation, competing objectives, and automated adversarial suffixes.<br>• <em>Prompt injection</em> — direct from the user, and critically <em>indirect</em> via retrieved documents, tool outputs, or uploaded files. Indirect injection is the one teams underexplore.<br>• <em>Data extraction</em> — system prompt leakage, training data extraction, cross-tenant data access.<br>• <em>Harmful content</em> across your policy categories.<br>• <em>Hallucination under pressure</em> — confidently asserted false premises, questions with no valid answer.<br>• <em>For agents</em> — inducing destructive actions, unauthorized tool use, budget exhaustion, and infinite loops.<br><br><strong>Method:</strong> combine manual expert probing (finds novel attack classes), automated red teaming (an LLM generating attack variants at scale, or gradient-based methods like GCG), and crowdsourced testing. Automated scales; humans find the categories automation does not know to try.<br><br><strong>Make it a process, not an event.</strong> Findings become permanent regression tests. Re-run before every model or prompt change, because fixes for one attack frequently open another and safety behavior shifts across model versions.<br><br><strong>Document coverage and residual risk honestly</strong> — red teaming demonstrates that specific attacks fail, never that the system is safe. Presenting it as proof of safety is the misuse to avoid.` },

{ q: 'How do you detect and measure hallucinations?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Distinguish two cases, because they need different methods.</strong><br><br><strong>Grounded generation (RAG, summarization) — the tractable case.</strong> The question is whether each claim is supported by the provided context, which is a checkable entailment problem:<br>• Decompose the output into atomic claims, then run an NLI model or LLM judge to check entailment against the source. Metrics like AlignScore, SummaC, and RAGAS faithfulness operationalize this.<br>• Require inline citations and <em>verify programmatically</em> that each cited span exists in the source. A quote that does not appear is a detectable hallucination — this converts attribution into an automated faithfulness check.<br><br><strong>Open-domain generation — much harder.</strong> No provided source to check against, so:<br>• <em>Self-consistency</em> — sample n responses at non-zero temperature and measure semantic agreement. High divergence indicates the model is confabulating; SelfCheckGPT formalizes this. It is the most reliable source-free signal available.<br>• <em>External verification</em> — retrieve evidence for each claim and check entailment.<br>• <em>Token-level uncertainty</em> — low probability on factual tokens correlates weakly with error, and is poorly calibrated on RLHF'd models. Treat as a weak signal.<br><br><strong>What does not work, and is worth stating:</strong> asking the model how confident it is. Verbalized confidence from an aligned model is poorly calibrated — it produces confident-sounding numbers that do not track accuracy.<br><br><strong>Also useless:</strong> ROUGE and BLEU. N-gram overlap cannot distinguish a faithful summary from a fluent fabrication using the same vocabulary.` },

{ q: 'What is adversarial testing for AI systems?', tags: [], a: `Adversarial testing probes the system with inputs designed to break it, rather than with representative traffic. It differs from red teaming in emphasis — red teaming targets harm and policy violations, adversarial testing targets <em>robustness and correctness</em> failures generally.<br><br><strong>Input perturbation:</strong> paraphrases, typos, casing changes, added whitespace, unicode lookalikes, and irrelevant context injected into the prompt. A model whose classification flips on a synonym is pattern-matching surface form rather than reasoning, and that fragility will surface in production.<br><br><strong>Semantic adversarial cases:</strong> false premises embedded in questions ("when did X acquire Y?" when no acquisition occurred), questions with no valid answer, contradictory instructions, and requests requiring information outside the provided context. These test whether the system abstains or confabulates.<br><br><strong>Boundary and scale cases:</strong> empty input, maximum-length input, wrong language, mixed languages, malformed structured input, and content that resembles instructions.<br><br><strong>For RAG specifically:</strong> queries whose answer is deliberately absent from the corpus, contradictory retrieved documents, and retrieved content containing injection payloads.<br><br><strong>Automated generation scales this:</strong> use an LLM to generate perturbations of your existing eval cases, or established libraries (TextAttack, CheckList-style behavioral testing).<br><br><strong>Measure invariance, not just accuracy:</strong> for semantically equivalent inputs, the output should be equivalent. Report the flip rate — how often a meaning-preserving change alters the answer — since aggregate accuracy hides fragility entirely.` },

{ q: 'How do you build a regression test suite for AI applications?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Source cases from real failures.</strong> Every production bug, every user complaint, and every red-team finding becomes a permanent test case. That is what makes the suite valuable — a synthetic set of easy examples proves nothing, while a curated set of things that actually broke catches recurrence.<br><br><strong>Design tests for non-determinism.</strong> Never assert exact output strings — they will flake and get disabled. Instead assert:<br>• <em>Invariants</em> — "response contains a citation", "no PII in output", "schema validates", "never called the delete tool".<br>• <em>Metric thresholds</em> — aggregate accuracy on a labeled set above a baseline.<br>• <em>Statistical pass rates</em> — run each case n times and require k successes, since a single run tells you little.<br><br><strong>Layer by cost:</strong> fast deterministic checks (schema validation, guardrails, safety cases) run on every commit. Expensive judge-based eval suites run per-PR or nightly. A suite too slow to run gets skipped.<br><br><strong>Gate on the right metrics:</strong> quality against baseline, cost per request (catch a change that triples spend), latency, and schema-failure rate.<br><br><strong>The AI-specific requirement:</strong> run the suite on a schedule against production config, not only on commits. Your model provider can change behavior with no commit on your side, and a scheduled run is the only thing that catches it.<br><br><strong>Version the suite alongside prompts and model config,</strong> so a result is always attributable to a specific configuration — otherwise you cannot tell whether a regression came from your change or from the environment.` },

{ q: 'What are benchmark suites like MMLU, HumanEval, GSM8K, and how do you interpret them?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>MMLU</strong> — 57 subjects of multiple-choice questions testing broad knowledge and reasoning. <strong>HumanEval</strong> — 164 Python problems evaluated by unit tests (pass@k). <strong>GSM8K</strong> — grade-school math word problems requiring multi-step arithmetic. Others worth knowing: MATH (competition math), MMLU-Pro and GPQA (harder, less saturated), SWE-bench (real GitHub issues), and BIG-bench.<br><br><strong>How to interpret them — the critical part:</strong><br>• <em>Contamination is pervasive.</em> These benchmarks are public and predate most model training cutoffs, so test items appear in training data. A high score may reflect memorization. This is the single biggest reason to distrust leaderboard numbers.<br>• <em>Saturation.</em> MMLU and HumanEval are near-ceiling for frontier models, so differences at the top are noise. Newer harder benchmarks (GPQA, SWE-bench) discriminate better.<br>• <em>Prompt sensitivity.</em> Reported scores vary several points with formatting, few-shot count, and answer extraction. Cross-paper comparisons are frequently not apples-to-apples.<br>• <em>Multiple-choice ≠ generation.</em> MMLU tests discrimination among given options, which is a much easier task than open-ended production.<br><br><strong>What they are actually good for:</strong> coarse capability screening and tracking progress across model generations. What they are not good for: predicting performance on <em>your</em> task.<br><br><strong>The practical stance:</strong> use benchmarks to build a shortlist, then evaluate candidates on your own labeled data. A model ranked fifth publicly regularly beats the top-ranked one on a specific domain application.` },

{ q: 'How do you evaluate a RAG system end to end?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Evaluate retrieval and generation separately — this is the most important structural decision.</strong> An end-to-end score tells you the system is bad, not which half to fix, and the two halves have entirely different remedies.<br><br><strong>Retrieval metrics</strong> (need labeled query→relevant-document pairs):<br>• <em>Context recall</em> — of the documents needed to answer, what fraction was retrieved? This is the hard ceiling on your system; anything not retrieved is unanswerable regardless of the model.<br>• <em>Context precision</em> — of what was retrieved, what fraction is relevant, and is it ranked highly? Low precision wastes tokens and triggers lost-in-the-middle.<br>• Standard IR metrics at your production k: Recall@k, MRR, NDCG@k.<br><br><strong>Generation metrics</strong> (given retrieved context):<br>• <em>Faithfulness</em> — is every claim supported by the context? Decompose into claims and check entailment. This is the hallucination metric.<br>• <em>Answer relevance</em> — does it address the question? A faithful answer can still be off-topic.<br>• <em>Answer correctness</em> versus ground truth where available.<br><br><strong>The diagnostic that follows:</strong> low recall → fix chunking, embeddings, hybrid search. High recall, low faithfulness → fix the grounding prompt. High faithfulness, low correctness → your corpus is wrong or incomplete.<br><br><strong>Tooling:</strong> RAGAS, TruLens, DeepEval implement these. Validate any judge-based metric against human labels first.<br><br><strong>Also evaluate abstention:</strong> include questions your corpus cannot answer and measure whether the system refuses rather than confabulating. Most eval sets omit these entirely and miss a major failure mode.` },

{ q: 'How do you evaluate the quality of AI agents?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Three levels, and only measuring the first is the common mistake.</strong><br><br><strong>Outcome:</strong> task success rate, verified programmatically where possible — tests pass, correct state change, expected artifact produced. Report it with cost per task, step count, and latency; success rate alone is misleading since an agent can succeed expensively.<br><br><strong>Trajectory:</strong> tool-selection accuracy, parameter correctness, redundant/repeated calls, error recovery rate, and adherence to constraints. This tells you <em>why</em> outcomes fail and is where actionable signal lives. Also check safety invariants — did it ever attempt a destructive action, exceed budget, or use a tool outside its mandate.<br><br><strong>Component:</strong> deterministic unit tests on the router, tool schemas, and retrieval, with mocked tools so they run in CI.<br><br><strong>Handle non-determinism explicitly:</strong> multiple runs per case with pass rate and variance reported, not a single number. And do not assert an exact trajectory — many paths are valid. Assert invariants and outcomes instead.<br><br><strong>The hardest part is task-success definition.</strong> For open-ended tasks ("research this topic") there is no programmatic check, so you need either a rubric-based judge validated against humans, or a redesign of the task into something checkable. Teams often discover the eval problem is really a specification problem.<br><br><strong>Build from production traces:</strong> mine real failures, oversample them, and maintain the set as a regression suite. Agent behavior shifts noticeably across model versions, so this must run continuously, not once.` },

{ q: 'What is the difference between offline and online evaluation?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Offline</strong> evaluation runs against a fixed labeled dataset, before deployment. Fast, cheap, repeatable, safe, and the right tool for iteration — you can test fifty prompt variants in an afternoon.<br><br><strong>Online</strong> evaluation measures behavior on live traffic: A/B tests, user feedback (thumbs, regeneration rate, abandonment), and downstream business metrics.<br><br><strong>Why you need both:</strong> offline datasets are a sample of a distribution that shifts, and they cannot capture what users actually do — real queries are messier, more ambiguous, and differently distributed than any curated set. Offline metrics also correlate imperfectly with user value: a response that scores well on a rubric may be too slow, too verbose, or subtly unhelpful in context.<br><br><strong>The correct workflow:</strong> offline evaluation to filter candidates down to those worth testing, then online evaluation to confirm real-world impact. Shipping every idea to an A/B test is slow and expensive; shipping on offline metrics alone means discovering the gap from users.<br><br><strong>Shadow mode bridges them:</strong> run the new version on live traffic without serving its output, log both, and compare offline. You get real distribution with zero user risk — the highest-value technique for risky changes.<br><br><strong>Close the loop:</strong> mine online failures back into the offline set. That feedback loop is what keeps offline evaluation representative over time, and without it your eval set slowly stops predicting production behavior.` },

{ q: 'How do you measure factual consistency in LLM outputs?', tags: [], a: `<strong>When you have a source (RAG, summarization), it is an entailment problem:</strong><br>• Decompose the output into atomic claims — a compound sentence can be half-supported, and sentence-level checking misses that.<br>• Check each claim against the source with an NLI model (entailment / contradiction / neutral) or an LLM judge with an explicit rubric.<br>• Report the fraction of claims entailed. Established metrics implementing this include AlignScore, SummaC, QAFactEval, and RAGAS faithfulness.<br><br><strong>The QA-based approach</strong> is a useful alternative: generate questions from the output, answer them from the source, and check agreement. It catches errors that direct entailment checking misses, particularly around quantities and entities.<br><br><strong>Citation verification is the cheapest strong signal:</strong> require the model to quote supporting spans verbatim, then string-match those quotes against the source. A quote that does not appear is a detected hallucination — deterministic, free, and no judge needed.<br><br><strong>Without a source:</strong> self-consistency (sample n outputs, measure semantic agreement — divergence indicates confabulation) or external retrieval-and-verify against a knowledge base.<br><br><strong>What does not work:</strong> ROUGE and BLEU cannot distinguish a faithful summary from a fluent fabrication reusing the same vocabulary. And asking the model for its confidence produces poorly calibrated numbers on RLHF'd models.<br><br><strong>Validate your measurement:</strong> whichever automated method you use, check its agreement with human factuality labels on a few hundred examples first. An unvalidated faithfulness metric can be uncorrelated with actual faithfulness.` },

{ q: 'How do you evaluate multi-turn conversation quality?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Single-turn metrics miss what matters in conversation.</strong> Each response can be individually good while the conversation fails — the system forgets a stated constraint, contradicts itself, or never resolves the user's goal.<br><br><strong>Conversation-level dimensions to measure:</strong><br>• <em>Goal completion</em> — did the user accomplish what they came for? The metric that actually matters, and the hardest to automate.<br>• <em>Memory/consistency</em> — does the system honor facts stated earlier? Test explicitly by planting a constraint at turn 2 and checking adherence at turn 20. Most systems fail this and never find out.<br>• <em>Self-consistency</em> — does it contradict its own earlier statements?<br>• <em>Topic-switch handling</em> — does it follow the user, or stay anchored to the previous subject?<br>• <em>Efficiency</em> — turns to resolution; a correct answer after 15 turns is a poor experience.<br><br><strong>Methodology:</strong> use scripted multi-turn test cases with defined checkpoints, and <em>simulated users</em> — an LLM playing a persona with a goal — to generate realistic multi-turn traffic at scale. Simulation is the practical way to get coverage, though it under-represents the messiness of real users.<br><br><strong>Handle the branching problem:</strong> the conversation path depends on the system's own responses, so you cannot fully script it. Define checkpoint assertions ("by turn 5, the constraint must be acknowledged") rather than exact transcripts.<br><br><strong>Online signals are especially valuable here:</strong> abandonment rate, escalation rate, and turns-to-resolution are direct measures of conversational failure that no offline set captures well.` },

{ q: 'What is the role of golden datasets in AI evaluation?', tags: [], a: `A golden dataset is a curated, human-verified set of inputs with known-correct outputs, serving as the stable reference against which everything else is measured.<br><br><strong>What it enables:</strong> comparing models and prompts on equal footing; regression detection when something changes; and — the most underrated use — <em>validating your automated metrics</em>. An LLM judge is only trustworthy if it agrees with human labels on a golden set. Without that, you are optimizing against an unvalidated proxy.<br><br><strong>Construction principles:</strong><br>• <em>Source from real traffic</em>, not invented examples. Real queries are messier and differently distributed than anything you would write.<br>• <em>Oversample failures and edge cases.</em> The average case is what the model already handles; the value is in the hard tail.<br>• <em>Human-verify every label.</em> A golden set with wrong labels is worse than none, because it actively misdirects optimization.<br>• <em>Cover the categories you care about</em> so you can report per-segment, not just aggregate.<br>• <em>Include negative cases</em> — unanswerable questions, out-of-scope requests, adversarial inputs. Most sets omit these and miss a major failure class.<br><br><strong>Keep it held out.</strong> Maintain a separate dev set for iteration; if you iterate against the golden set, you overfit to it and it stops measuring generalization. That degradation is invisible — the numbers keep improving.<br><br><strong>Maintain it over time:</strong> add production failures continuously, and periodically re-verify labels, since correct answers can change as your product and data change.` },

{ q: 'How do you implement continuous evaluation in production?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Three complementary mechanisms:</strong><br><br><strong>1. Scheduled offline runs</strong> of your eval suite against production configuration — not just on commits. This is the AI-specific requirement: your provider can change model behavior with no commit on your side, so a nightly run against the live config is the only thing that catches it. Alert on regression against baseline.<br><br><strong>2. Online sampling with automated scoring.</strong> Sample a percentage of live traffic and run automated evaluators — groundedness checks for RAG, schema validity, guardrail triggers, and an LLM judge on a smaller sample. This measures the real distribution rather than your curated set, which is what catches distribution shift.<br><br><strong>3. Implicit user signals</strong> collected continuously: thumbs down rate, regeneration rate, conversation abandonment, escalation to human. Regeneration rate is a strong quality proxy that costs nothing to collect and is underused.<br><br><strong>Alert on trend, not thresholds.</strong> Quality drift is gradual, so an absolute threshold either fires constantly or never. Compare rolling windows against a baseline and alert on statistically significant movement.<br><br><strong>Close the loop:</strong> low-scoring production samples get reviewed and added to the eval set. That is what keeps offline evaluation representative — without it your set slowly stops predicting production behavior.<br><br><strong>Watch the leading indicators:</strong> schema-failure rate and guardrail-trigger rate spike immediately on a model or prompt change, well before quality metrics move. They are the cheapest early warning available.` },

{ q: 'How do you evaluate bias in AI model outputs?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Define what fairness means for your application first</strong> — the metrics are mathematically incompatible, so this is a decision you must make explicitly rather than discover.<br><br><strong>The main definitions:</strong> <em>demographic parity</em> (equal positive rates across groups), <em>equalized odds</em> (equal true-positive and false-positive rates), <em>equal opportunity</em> (equal true-positive rates), and <em>calibration</em> (predicted probabilities mean the same thing across groups). Except in degenerate cases you cannot satisfy demographic parity and calibration simultaneously — that is a proven impossibility result, not an engineering shortfall.<br><br><strong>Measurement methods:</strong><br>• <em>Counterfactual testing</em> — hold everything constant and vary only the demographic signal (swap names, pronouns, stated group membership) and measure output differences. The cleanest causal evidence of bias, and directly actionable.<br>• <em>Disaggregated performance</em> — report every quality metric per group. Aggregate accuracy routinely hides that one group is served badly.<br>• <em>Benchmark suites</em> — BBQ, StereoSet, CrowS-Pairs, WinoBias for stereotype and association testing.<br>• <em>Generation analysis</em> — sentiment, toxicity, and topic distribution conditioned on group mentions.<br><br><strong>Test intersectionally.</strong> A model can pass on gender and on race separately while failing badly for a specific intersection — this is common, and single-axis testing systematically misses it. Sample sizes get small, so plan the data collection.<br><br><strong>Make it continuous:</strong> bias emerges from data drift and model updates, so re-audit on a schedule and after every change, not once at launch.` },

{ q: 'How do you compare two models or prompts with statistical rigor?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Account for non-determinism:</strong> run each variant multiple times per example. A single run conflates sampling noise with real difference, and this is the most common methodological error.<br><br><strong>Use paired comparison.</strong> Evaluate both variants on the <em>same</em> examples and analyze the paired differences — this removes example difficulty as a variance source and dramatically increases statistical power for a given sample size. A paired t-test or Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-example differences beats comparing two independent means.<br><br><strong>Compute the sample size in advance</strong> from your expected effect size and variance. If you need to detect a 2-point difference and per-example variance is high, you may need many hundreds of examples — knowing this before running prevents the most common failure, which is:<br><br><strong>Do not peek and stop when it looks significant.</strong> Repeatedly testing as data accumulates inflates false positives severely. Either fix the sample size in advance, or use sequential testing methods designed for it.<br><br><strong>Report confidence intervals, not just p-values.</strong> "B is 3.2 points better (95% CI: 0.4–6.0)" is far more useful than "p &lt; 0.05" — it conveys the effect size and the uncertainty, which is what you actually need to make a decision.<br><br><strong>Correct for multiple comparisons</strong> when testing several variants — Bonferroni or Benjamini-Hochberg. Testing ten prompts and picking the best without correction reliably selects noise.<br><br><strong>Then ask whether the difference matters:</strong> statistical significance is not practical significance. A significant 0.5-point gain at double the cost is a loss.` },

{ q: 'How do you evaluate robustness across input variations?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Measure invariance, not just accuracy.</strong> For semantically equivalent inputs the output should be equivalent, so the metric is the <em>flip rate</em> — how often a meaning-preserving perturbation changes the answer. Aggregate accuracy hides fragility entirely: a model can score 92% while flipping on a third of paraphrases.<br><br><strong>Perturbation categories to test:</strong><br>• <em>Surface</em> — typos, casing, extra whitespace, punctuation, unicode lookalikes.<br>• <em>Paraphrase</em> — same meaning, different wording. The most important category, and where fragility is most damaging.<br>• <em>Structural</em> — reordering independent instructions, changing where the question sits in the prompt, varying few-shot example order.<br>• <em>Distractor injection</em> — adding irrelevant context, which should not change the answer but frequently does.<br>• <em>Length</em> — very short and very long inputs.<br>• <em>Language and formality</em> where applicable.<br><br><strong>Generate at scale with an LLM:</strong> produce perturbations of existing eval cases automatically, verifying that meaning is preserved. This turns a 100-case eval set into a robustness suite cheaply.<br><br><strong>Interpret the results correctly:</strong> high sensitivity means the model is pattern-matching surface form rather than reasoning about the task. The fixes are constraining the output space, adding few-shot examples covering the variation, making the decision criteria explicit, or fine-tuning — not prompt tinkering.<br><br><strong>Second-order implication:</strong> sensitivity to wording implies sensitivity to weight changes, so a fragile system will shift when the provider updates the model. Pin versions and monitor.` },

{ q: 'What differs between evaluating traditional ML and LLM applications?', tags: ['FREQUENTLY ASKED'], a: `<strong>Ground truth.</strong> Traditional ML has labeled data with unambiguous correct answers, so accuracy, F1, and AUC are well-defined. LLM output is often open-ended with many valid answers, so evaluation requires rubrics, judges, or human review — all noisy and requiring their own validation.<br><br><strong>Determinism.</strong> A trained classifier gives the same output for the same input, so tests are exact assertions. LLM output varies, so tests must be statistical — pass rates over multiple runs with reported variance.<br><br><strong>Output space.</strong> Classification has a finite label set; generation has an effectively infinite output space, which makes exhaustive testing impossible and edge-case discovery much harder.<br><br><strong>The artifact under test.</strong> Traditional ML evaluates a model you trained. LLM applications evaluate a <em>system</em> — prompt, retrieval, tools, model — where the model is often a third-party dependency you did not train and cannot inspect.<br><br><strong>Dependency drift.</strong> Your provider can change the model with no commit on your side. Continuous scheduled evaluation is a reliability requirement, not a nice-to-have — there is no analogue in traditional ML where your artifact is frozen.<br><br><strong>Failure modes.</strong> Hallucination, prompt injection, and jailbreaks have no traditional-ML counterpart, and they fail silently — right status code, wrong content.<br><br><strong>Cost.</strong> Evaluation itself is expensive: judge calls and multiple runs per case mean eval spend can be a meaningful fraction of total cost.<br><br><strong>What stays the same:</strong> data quality determines outcomes, held-out sets prevent overfitting, and per-segment reporting beats aggregate.` },

{ q: 'How do you set up an evaluation framework from scratch?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>1. Define success concretely, in writing.</strong> Not "good answers" — specific, checkable criteria per task type. This forced specificity is where most of the value comes from, and much of what looks like a modeling problem turns out to be an undefined-requirements problem.<br><br><strong>2. Collect real examples.</strong> 50–200 inputs from actual or realistic traffic, deliberately oversampling edge cases, failures, and adversarial inputs. Not invented examples — real distribution matters.<br><br><strong>3. Label them.</strong> Human-verified expected outputs or acceptance criteria. Domain experts where the domain requires it.<br><br><strong>4. Split dev and held-out.</strong> Iterate against dev; validate on held-out. Without this you overfit the prompt to the examples you keep reading, and the degradation is invisible.<br><br><strong>5. Pick metrics by task type</strong> — deterministic where possible (exact match, schema validity, code execution), judges only where necessary.<br><br><strong>6. Validate the judges</strong> against human labels before trusting them. This step is routinely skipped and invalidates everything downstream.<br><br><strong>7. Establish a baseline</strong> with the simplest approach, so every subsequent change is measured against something.<br><br><strong>8. Wire into CI</strong> — gate on quality, cost, and latency; run on a schedule against production config too.<br><br><strong>9. Close the loop</strong> — production failures flow back into the set continuously.<br><br><strong>The sequencing advice:</strong> do this <em>before</em> writing prompts. Teams that build evals after shipping spend months unable to tell whether changes help, and every improvement is anecdotal.` },

{ q: 'Your model passes one fairness metric but fails another. How do you handle conflicting audits?', tags: ['TRADEOFF'], a: `<strong>Lead with the mathematics: this is expected, not a bug.</strong> Kleinberg et al. and Chouldechova proved that demographic parity, equalized odds, and calibration cannot generally be satisfied simultaneously when base rates differ across groups. No amount of engineering resolves it — the conflict is structural.<br><br><strong>So the task is choosing which definition your application requires,</strong> and that is a normative decision informed by domain and law, not a technical optimization.<br><br><strong>How the choice maps to context:</strong><br>• <em>Equal opportunity</em> (equal true-positive rates) fits when the cost of missing a qualified candidate dominates — hiring, lending, admissions.<br>• <em>Calibration</em> fits when scores are consumed as probabilities by downstream decision-makers — a risk score that means different things for different groups actively misleads.<br>• <em>Demographic parity</em> fits when representation itself is the goal, or where legal frameworks impose it.<br>• <em>Equalized odds</em> when both error types carry serious consequences.<br><br><strong>Then be explicit and document it:</strong> state which definition you optimized, why, what you traded away, and the measured disparity on the other metrics. An audit trail showing a reasoned choice is defensible; silently optimizing whichever metric looks best is not.<br><br><strong>Involve the right people</strong> — legal, domain experts, and affected stakeholders. This is not an engineering decision to make alone.<br><br><strong>The productive reframe:</strong> often the metric conflict reveals that the <em>base rates</em> differ because of upstream structural inequity. Fixing the data collection, the label definition, or the decision process may address the root cause better than tuning a threshold.` },

{ q: 'Your model was fair at deployment but biased 6 months later. How do you monitor continuously?', tags: [], a: `<strong>Diagnose which drift occurred, because the fixes differ:</strong><br>• <em>Data drift</em> — the input distribution shifted (different applicant population, changed marketing channels), so the model now operates off its validation distribution.<br>• <em>Concept drift</em> — the relationship between features and outcomes changed.<br>• <em>Feedback loops</em> — the model's own decisions shaped subsequent training data. This is the most insidious: rejecting a group means you observe fewer positive outcomes for them, which "confirms" the model on retraining and compounds.<br>• <em>Model or pipeline change</em> — a retraining run, a feature change, or a provider model update.<br><br><strong>Monitoring design:</strong> track fairness metrics as first-class production metrics, computed on a schedule and disaggregated by group <em>and intersection</em>. Alert on trend against the deployment baseline, not on absolute thresholds — drift is gradual.<br><br><strong>Also monitor upstream:</strong> input distribution per group, feature drift, and outcome base rates. These move before the fairness metric does, giving you lead time.<br><br><strong>Break feedback loops explicitly:</strong> reserve a small randomized holdout that bypasses the model, so you keep collecting unbiased outcome data. Without it, your training data is a record of your own past decisions and the loop cannot be detected, let alone broken.<br><br><strong>Process requirements:</strong> re-audit after every model or data-pipeline change, retain historical predictions with features for retrospective analysis, and assign clear ownership — continuous monitoring without an owner degrades into an unread dashboard.` },

{ q: 'An external auditor cannot reproduce your results. How do you ensure audit reproducibility?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>Identify the sources of non-reproducibility, then eliminate them one by one:</strong><br><br><strong>Version everything as one artifact:</strong> model version (pinned, not a floating alias), prompt version, sampling parameters, retrieval config, code, and the evaluation dataset. A result is only meaningful attached to a complete configuration hash. Partial versioning is the most common cause of "we can't reproduce it."<br><br><strong>Pin the model explicitly.</strong> If you called a floating endpoint six months ago, the weights behind it may no longer exist — that result is permanently unreproducible. For anything auditable, use dated snapshots and record deprecation dates.<br><br><strong>Handle inherent non-determinism honestly:</strong> temperature 0 reduces but does not eliminate variance (GPU batching and floating-point non-associativity can flip near-ties). So report distributions from multiple runs with seeds recorded, rather than claiming a single deterministic number. Documenting the expected variance <em>is</em> the reproducible result.<br><br><strong>Preserve the data:</strong> the exact evaluation set, immutable and versioned; and for production decisions, the full input including retrieved context, since a decision cannot be reproduced without what the model actually saw.<br><br><strong>Log at decision time, not after.</strong> Store the rendered prompt, model version, config hash, output, and timestamp for every consequential decision. Reconstructing this retrospectively is impossible.<br><br><strong>Package the environment</strong> — containerized, dependency-locked — and provide the auditor a runnable harness rather than a description.<br><br><strong>The honest caveat to state:</strong> for third-party API models, exact reproducibility is not fully achievable. Document that limitation explicitly rather than implying a guarantee you cannot make.` },

{ q: 'How do you structure red teaming for a chatbot before launch?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>1. Define scope and threat model.</strong> What harms matter for <em>this</em> product? A children's education bot and an internal engineering assistant have different threat surfaces. Write the policy you are testing against — you cannot red team without a definition of violation.<br><br><strong>2. Assemble a diverse team.</strong> Include people outside the build team, domain experts for specialized content, and — importantly — people from the demographics your system might harm. Homogeneous teams reliably miss category-specific failures.<br><br><strong>3. Cover the attack taxonomy systematically</strong> rather than ad hoc: jailbreaks (persona, hypothetical, gradual escalation, encoding, low-resource languages), direct and <em>indirect</em> prompt injection, system-prompt extraction, PII and training-data extraction, harmful content per policy category, hallucination under adversarial pressure, and — if the bot has tools — unauthorized actions and budget exhaustion.<br><br><strong>4. Combine manual and automated.</strong> Automated red teaming (an LLM generating attack variants, or gradient-based methods) provides scale and coverage; human experts find the novel categories automation does not know to try. You need both.<br><br><strong>5. Triage findings by severity and exploitability,</strong> fix, and — critically — <strong>convert every finding into a permanent regression test.</strong> A fix without a test regresses on the next model update.<br><br><strong>6. Re-run before every model or prompt change,</strong> since safety behavior shifts across versions and fixes for one attack often open another.<br><br><strong>7. Document coverage and residual risk honestly.</strong> Red teaming shows specific attacks fail; it never proves safety. Presenting it as a safety guarantee is the misuse to avoid, and reviewers notice.` },

{ q: 'How do you red team a multimodal model where text-only tests miss cross-modal attacks?', tags: ['CRITICAL'], a: `<strong>The core insight is that images are an instruction channel.</strong> A vision model reads text in an image as text, so any safety filtering applied only to the text input is trivially bypassed by rendering the same prompt as a picture. Teams that treat images as inert data miss this entirely.<br><br><strong>Cross-modal attack classes to test:</strong><br>• <em>Text-in-image injection</em> — instructions rendered visually, including as faint or low-contrast text a human skims past but the model reads.<br>• <em>Split payloads</em> — benign text plus benign-looking image that combine into a harmful request, so neither modality's filter fires.<br>• <em>Indirect injection via visual content</em> — a screenshot, document scan, or web page image containing instructions, which is the agent-relevant case and the most dangerous.<br>• <em>Adversarial perturbations</em> — imperceptible pixel modifications that steer model behavior; the continuous input space makes gradient-based attacks far more tractable than on discrete text.<br>• <em>Typographic attacks</em> — text in the image contradicting the actual visual content, which reliably fools classification.<br>• <em>Steganographic and encoded content.</em><br><br><strong>What this means for defenses:</strong> run OCR on images and apply your text safety filters to the extracted text, not just to the user's typed message. Apply injection classifiers to <em>all</em> modalities. And for agents, treat visual input from external sources as untrusted exactly as you treat retrieved web pages.<br><br><strong>Evaluate jointly, not per modality.</strong> A test suite that checks text safety and image safety separately provably misses combination attacks — the combinations are the point.` },
]};
